From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <address-in-upstream-commit@localhost>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 23:10:32 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 27/37] arm64: dts: apple: t6050: share the K boards' N1 and
 USB-C ports, add K114s and K116c

The K114s and K116c had no USB, no Wi-Fi/Bluetooth and no PCIe: the
shared t6050-k114-k116.dtsi disabled ATC1, nub-spmi-a0 and the PCIe host,
and only the K114c and K116s described their N1 port and USB-C ports,
each in its own copy.

The boards' ADTs have the same apcie0 port 0 (registers, IRQ 1523, PERST#
GPIO 80, N1 on dart-apcie0 streams 1-3), the same PD controllers (hpm1 at
0xa on nub-spmi-a0, hpm2 at 0xc on nub-spmi-a1, SN201202x) and the same
atc-phy, usb-drd and dart-usb nodes on all four boards. So:

- t6050-k114-k116.dtsi now describes PCIe port 0 with the N1, its
  wifi0/bluetooth0 aliases and PERST# pin 80. The board file defines the
  N1's firmware name and its board-id before including it; the platform
  ID is board-id << 16 | 0x6050.
- The new t6050-k114-k116-usb.dtsi describes ATC1 (left-front) and ATC2
  (right): PD controllers, combo PHYs, DWC3s, DARTs and the atcphy
  aliases. Boards include it after t6050-peripherals.dtsi, which defines
  nub-spmi-a1 for them.
- K114c and K116s keep only their deltas: K114c the nub-spmi-a0 fault
  queue window, the MagSafe PD controller and the ATC2 router; K116s the
  SD reader on port 1 and the single nub-spmi-a1 window.
- K114s (board-id 0x14) and K116c (board-id 0x06) include both.

The K114c and K116s DTBs are byte-identical before and after, as are
the other T6050 DTBs. The shared blocks are Joshua Warren's K114c and
Jens Axboe's K116s descriptions.

Signed-off-by: Ryan Murray <address-in-upstream-commit@localhost>

[re-cut onto the K114s r3d tree]
